NAME
       mke2fs - create an ext2, ext3 or ext4 file system

SYNOPSIS
       mke2fs [-t TYPE] [-L LABEL] [-b BLOCK-SIZE] [-m PERCENT] [-F] [-q] [-n]
              DEVICE [SIZE]
       mkfs.ext4 [OPTIONS] DEVICE

DESCRIPTION
       mke2fs writes a new ext2, ext3 or ext4 file system on a device. The
       name it is called by picks the type: mkfs.ext4 makes ext4, mkfs.ext3
       ext3, mkfs.ext2 ext2. Called as plain mke2fs without -t, it makes
       ext2 (the default in /etc/mke2fs.conf), which has no journal, so give
       -t ext4 or use mkfs.ext4. Everything that was on the device is lost.

       ext4 can grow while mounted and can also shrink while unmounted
       (resize2fs). Labels are at most 16 characters.

       When the device already holds a file system, mke2fs shows what it
       found and asks "Proceed anyway? (y,N)". Answer y only when you are
       sure.

       Part of the space (5% by default) is reserved for root, so that the
       system keeps working when users fill the disk; df counts it as used.

OPTIONS
       -t TYPE
              The file system type: ext2, ext3 or ext4.

       -L LABEL
              Set the volume label (up to 16 characters).

       -b BLOCK-SIZE
              Block size in bytes: 1024, 2048 or 4096.

       -m PERCENT
              Reserve this percentage of the blocks for root (default 5).

       -F     Force: do not ask, even when the device holds a file system.

       -q     Quiet: print less.

       -n     Dry run: show what would be done without writing anything.

       -U UUID
              Use this UUID instead of a random one.

EXAMPLES
       Make an ext4 file system on /dev/vdb2 and mount it at /archive at
       every boot:

           mkfs.ext4 /dev/vdb2
           mkdir /archive
           blkid /dev/vdb2
           echo 'UUID=<the UUID shown> /archive ext4 defaults 0 0' >> /etc/fstab
           mount -a

       The same with mke2fs and a label:

           mke2fs -t ext4 -L archive /dev/vdb2

       Rename the label of an existing ext4 file system:

           e2label /dev/vdb2 backups

FILES
       /etc/mke2fs.conf
              Default settings for each file system type.

SEE ALSO
       mkfs(8), mkfs.xfs(8), resize2fs(8), e2label(8), tune2fs(8), e2fsck(8),
       blkid(8), mount(8)
